\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Open sets generate the Borel algebra}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{A difference system is a family of Borel sets that
contains #{\Sigma}, contains #{A\setminus B} whenever it
contains #{A} and #{B} with #{B\subset A}, and contains
increasing unions. The smallest difference system containing
the open sets is the Borel #{\sigma}-algebra. The Borel
probability of \ref{fdip-00EV} is the only one with the
stated cylinder masses.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{D} be the intersection of every difference system
that contains the open sets. For an open set #{U}, the
members #{B\in D} with #{U\cap B\in D} form a difference
system: intersection with #{U} preserves differences and
increasing unions, and it contains every open set because
the intersection of two open sets is open. That system
equals #{D}. The same step with this #{B} fixed shows that
#{D} is closed under intersections. A complement is a
difference from #{\Sigma}. A finite union follows by
complements and intersections, and a countable union is an
increasing union of finite unions. Thus #{D} is a
#{\sigma}-algebra containing the open sets, so #{D}
contains every Borel set.}

\p{An open set in #{\Sigma} is a countable union of
cylinders. Successive differences in the algebra of
\ref{fdip-00EV} make that union disjoint. Countable
additivity then gives both probabilities the same mass, so
they agree on every open set. The sets on which they agree contain #{\Sigma},
contain nested differences, and contain increasing unions.
That difference system contains the open sets, hence
contains #{D}. The two probabilities agree on every Borel
set.}
}
